<div class="container-fluid dns-page">
  <%# What it is and how it's doing %>
  <div class="card mb-4 mt-3" id="dns-status">
    <div class="card-header d-flex align-items-center justify-content-between">
      <h5 class="mb-0"><%= lucide_icon('globe', size: 18) %> DNS and DHCP for your network</h5>
      <% if !@installed %>
        <span class="badge bg-secondary">Not installed</span>
      <% elsif @mode == 'off' %>
        <span class="badge bg-secondary">Off</span>
      <% elsif @running && @check && @check[:local] && @check[:outside] %>
        <span class="badge bg-success">Answering</span>
      <% elsif @running %>
        <span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">Not answering</span>
      <% else %>
        <span class="badge bg-danger">Stopped</span>
      <% end %>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="text-muted">
        The NAS can be your network's DNS server, the service that turns names like <code>example.com</code>
        into addresses. Devices ask the NAS, and it asks the provider you pick over an encrypted connection,
        so your internet provider can't read or change the answers. It checks the signatures domains put on
        their answers (DNSSEC), and refuses an answer whose signature doesn't match. It remembers
        answers so repeat lookups are fast, keeps no record of who looked up what, and gives the NAS and your
        devices names like <code><%= @nas_name %></code>. It can also hand out addresses to your devices (DHCP),
        in place of your router.
      </p>

      <% if !@installed %>
        <p class="text-muted small">It needs Unbound, a DNS server from Ubuntu. Installing it changes nothing on your network: DNS stays off until you turn it on below.</p>
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" data-call="openInstallTerminal" data-args='["unbound"]'>Install Unbound</button>
      <% elsif @mode != 'off' %>
        <dl class="dns-facts mb-0">
          <dt>Devices use</dt>
          <dd><%= @addresses.any? ? @addresses.join(', ') : 'no IPv4 address on this interface' %></dd>
          <dt>Local names</dt>
          <dd>
            <% if @check.nil? %>
              <span class="text-danger">Unbound isn't running.</span>
            <% elsif @check[:local] %>
              ✓ Answered in <%= @check[:local] %> ms
            <% else %>
              <span class="text-danger">✗ No answer for the NAS's own name.</span>
            <% end %>
          </dd>
          <dt>Outside names</dt>
          <dd>
            <% if @check.nil? %>
              <span class="text-danger">Unbound isn't running.</span>
            <% elsif @check[:outside] %>
              ✓ Answered in <%= @check[:outside] %> ms, encrypted, through <%= @provider == 'custom' ? 'your server' : DnsService::PROVIDERS.dig(@provider, :name) %>
            <% else %>
              <span class="text-danger">✗ No answer from <%= @provider == 'custom' ? 'your server' : DnsService::PROVIDERS.dig(@provider, :name) %>. Is the internet up? Try another provider.</span>
            <% end %>
          </dd>
          <% if @totals && @totals['queries'].to_i.positive? %>
            <dt>Lookups</dt>
            <dd>
              <%= number_with_delimiter(@totals['queries']) %> in <%= distance_of_time_in_words(Time.at(@totals['since'].to_i), Time.now) %>;
              <%= (100.0 * @totals['answered_here'].to_i / @totals['queries'].to_i).round %>% answered on the NAS (its cache, its names
              and its blocklist), without asking <%= @provider == 'custom' ? 'your server' : DnsService::PROVIDERS.dig(@provider, :name) %>
            </dd>
            <dt>Signed answers</dt>
            <dd>
              <%= number_with_delimiter(@totals['secure']) %> came signed by their domain, and the signature checked out<% if @totals['bogus'].to_i.positive? %>.
                <%= number_with_delimiter(@totals['bogus']) %> failed the check, so the NAS refused <%= @totals['bogus'].to_i == 1 ? 'it' : 'them' %> rather
                than risk passing on a faked address; that's usually a domain whose signing is broken, not an attack<% end %>
            </dd>
          <% end %>
          <% if @mode == 'router' %>
            <dt>Internet</dt>
            <dd>
              <% if @router_state.nil? %>
                <span class="text-danger">✗ The router's rules aren't in place. Save again, and if it's refused, the message says why.</span>
              <% elsif @wan_addresses.empty? %>
                <span class="text-warning">No address on <%= @wan_interface %> yet: the provider hasn't answered. Is the cable in?</span>
              <% else %>
                ✓ Through <%= @wan_interface %> at <%= @wan_addresses.join(', ') %><%= ", the provider's router #{@default_route.last}" if @default_route&.first == @wan_interface %>;
                <%= pluralize(@forwards.size, 'port') %> let in
              <% end %>
            </dd>
          <% end %>
          <% if @mode == 'dns_dhcp' || @mode == 'router' %>
            <dt>Addresses</dt>
            <dd>
              <% if @dnsmasq_running %>
                ✓ Handing out <%= @prefix %>.<%= @dhcp[:first] %> to <%= @prefix %>.<%= @dhcp[:last] %>;
                <a href="<%= network_index_path %>"><%= pluralize(@leases.size, 'device') %></a> <%= @leases.size == 1 ? 'has' : 'have' %> one
              <% else %>
                <span class="text-danger">✗ dnsmasq isn't running, so devices can't get an address.</span>
              <% end %>
            </dd>
          <% end %>
        </dl>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# The settings %>
  <form action="<%= dns_update_path %>" method="post" id="dns-settings">
    <input type="hidden" name="_method" value="put">
    <input type="hidden" name="authenticity_token" value="<%= form_authenticity_token %>">

    <div class="card mb-4">
      <div class="card-header"><h5 class="mb-0"><%= lucide_icon('settings', size: 18) %> Settings</h5></div>
      <div class="card-body">
        <h6>DNS</h6>
        <div class="dns-choices mb-4">
          <label class="dns-choice">
            <input type="radio" name="mode" value="off" <%= 'checked' if @mode == 'off' %>>
            <span class="dns-choice-title">Off</span>
            <span class="dns-choice-text">Nothing listens. Devices keep using your router's DNS.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="dns-choice">
            <input type="radio" name="mode" value="dns" <%= 'checked' if @mode == 'dns' %> <%= 'disabled' unless @installed %>>
            <span class="dns-choice-title">DNS for my network <span class="badge bg-success ms-1">Recommended</span></span>
            <span class="dns-choice-text">The NAS answers DNS. Your router keeps handing out addresses; point its DNS setting at the NAS (below), or one device's.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="dns-choice">
            <input type="radio" name="mode" value="dns_dhcp" <%= 'checked' if @mode == 'dns_dhcp' %> <%= 'disabled' unless @installed %>>
            <span class="dns-choice-title">DNS + DHCP <span class="badge bg-secondary ms-1">Advanced</span></span>
            <span class="dns-choice-text">The NAS also hands out addresses, in place of your router. Turn your router's DHCP off first: the NAS won't start while another DHCP server answers.</span>
          </label>
          <label class="dns-choice">
            <input type="radio" name="mode" value="router" <%= 'checked' if @mode == 'router' %> <%= 'disabled' unless @installed %>>
            <span class="dns-choice-title">Router <span class="badge bg-secondary ms-1">Advanced</span></span>
            <span class="dns-choice-text">DNS + DHCP, and the NAS is the way to the internet: a second network interface takes the provider's connection, and your devices reach the internet through the NAS. Your router becomes an access point.</span>
          </label>
        </div>

        <%# Shown while Router is picked (dns.css) %>
        <div class="dns-router mb-4">
          <h6>Internet</h6>
          <p class="text-muted small">The interface the provider's connection is plugged into. It takes an address from the provider, and nothing from the internet reaches the NAS through it except the ports you let in below. The firewall has to be on first (Network → Security).</p>
          <select name="wan_interface" class="form-select mb-2 dns-interface" aria-label="Internet interface">
            <option value="">Choose the internet interface</option>
            <% @interfaces.each do |name, addresses| %>
              <option value="<%= name %>" <%= 'selected' if name == @wan_interface %>><%= name %> (<%= addresses.reject(&:link_local?).map(&:to_s).join(', ').presence || 'no address' %>)</option>
            <% end %>
          </select>
          <p class="text-muted small mb-0"><strong>The way back:</strong> set this page to DNS + DHCP or Off, then move the provider's cable back to your router. While the NAS is off or restarting, the house has no internet.</p>
        </div>

        <%# Shown while DNS + DHCP is picked (dns.css) %>
        <div class="dns-dhcp mb-4">
          <h6>DHCP</h6>
          <% if !@dnsmasq_installed %>
            <p class="text-muted small">Handing out addresses needs dnsmasq, from Ubuntu. Installing it changes nothing on your network until you save with DNS + DHCP.</p>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" data-call="openInstallTerminal" data-args='["dnsmasq"]'>Install dnsmasq</button>
          <% else %>
            <p class="text-muted small">On the interface's network, where the NAS is <%= @addresses.first || 'not yet' %>. Devices get the NAS as their DNS server, and <code><%= @domain %></code> as their domain. Fixed addresses for chosen devices are on <a href="<%= network_hosts_path %>">Network → Hosts</a>.</p>
            <div class="row g-3 dns-dhcp-fields">
              <div class="col-md-6">
                <label class="form-label small" for="dhcp-first">Addresses handed out</label>
                <div class="input-group">
                  <span class="input-group-text"><%= @prefix %>.</span>
                  <input type="number" class="form-control" id="dhcp-first" name="dhcp_first" value="<%= @dhcp[:first] %>" min="2" max="254">
                  <span class="input-group-text">to <%= @prefix %>.</span>
                  <input type="number" class="form-control" id="dhcp-last" name="dhcp_last" value="<%= @dhcp[:last] %>" min="2" max="254" aria-label="Last address">
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="col-md-3 dns-not-router">
                <label class="form-label small" for="dhcp-router">Router</label>
                <div class="input-group">
                  <span class="input-group-text"><%= @prefix %>.</span>
                  <input type="number" class="form-control" id="dhcp-router" name="dhcp_router" value="<%= @dhcp[:router] %>" min="1" max="254">
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="col-md-3">
                <label class="form-label small" for="dhcp-lease-time">Devices keep an address for</label>
                <select class="form-select" id="dhcp-lease-time" name="lease_time">
                  <% DnsmasqService::LEASE_TIMES.each do |label, seconds| %>
                    <option value="<%= seconds %>" <%= 'selected' if seconds == @dhcp[:lease_time] %>><%= label %></option>
                  <% end %>
                </select>
              </div>
            </div>
          <% end %>
        </div>

        <h6>Provider</h6>
        <p class="text-muted small">Where the NAS sends the lookups it can't answer itself. All of them are free, need no account, encrypt the connection and check DNSSEC.</p>
        <div class="dns-choices dns-providers mb-4">
          <% DnsService::PROVIDERS.each do |key, provider| %>
            <label class="dns-choice">
              <input type="radio" name="provider" value="<%= key %>" <%= 'checked' if @provider == key %>>
              <span class="dns-choice-title"><%= provider[:name] %><%= ' (default)' if key == DnsService::DEFAULT_PROVIDER %></span>
              <span class="dns-choice-text"><%= provider[:about] %></span>
            </label>
          <% end %>
          <label class="dns-choice">
            <input type="radio" name="provider" value="custom" id="dns-provider-custom" <%= 'checked' if @provider == 'custom' %>>
            <span class="dns-choice-title">Another server</span>
            <span class="dns-choice-text">Any DNS-over-TLS server: its addresses and the name on its certificate.</span>
          </label>
          <div class="dns-custom">
            <div class="row g-2">
              <div class="col-md-7">
                <label class="form-label small" for="dns-custom-addresses">Addresses (one to four, IPv4 or IPv6)</label>
                <input type="text" class="form-control" id="dns-custom-addresses" name="custom_addresses" value="<%= @custom_addresses %>" placeholder="9.9.9.9 149.112.112.112">
              </div>
              <div class="col-md-5">
                <label class="form-label small" for="dns-custom-tls-name">TLS name</label>
                <input type="text" class="form-control" id="dns-custom-tls-name" name="custom_tls_name" value="<%= @custom_tls_name %>" placeholder="dns.quad9.net">
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h6>Network interface</h6>
        <p class="text-muted small">DNS answers on this one, for the devices on its network (and on Tailscale, when it's on).</p>
        <select name="interface" class="form-select mb-4 dns-interface" aria-label="Network interface">
          <% @interfaces.each do |name, addresses| %>
            <option value="<%= name %>" <%= 'selected' if name == @interface %>><%= name %> (<%= addresses.reject(&:link_local?).map(&:to_s).join(', ').presence || 'no address' %>)</option>
          <% end %>
        </select>

        <h6>IPv6</h6>
        <div class="form-check mb-1">
          <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" name="ipv6_advertise" value="1" id="dns-ipv6-advertise" <%= 'checked' if @ipv6_advertise %>>
          <label class="form-check-label" for="dns-ipv6-advertise">Announce the NAS as the IPv6 DNS server <span class="badge bg-secondary ms-1">Advanced</span></label>
        </div>
        <p class="text-muted small mb-2">
          Devices using IPv6 learn their DNS server from the network's announcements, usually your router's. With this on,
          the NAS announces itself too, at <code><%= @own_ipv6_address %></code>, an address of its own that stays the same
          whatever your internet provider hands out. It never announces itself as a router. Better still, if your router
          lets you set the IPv6 DNS server it announces (OpenWrt does), set it to the NAS there; see IPv6, below.
        </p>
        <% unless @radvd_installed %>
          <div class="dns-radvd mb-2">
            <p class="text-muted small mb-2">Announcing needs radvd, from Ubuntu. Installing it changes nothing until you save with this ticked.</p>
            <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary" data-call="openInstallTerminal" data-args='["radvd"]'>Install radvd</button>
          </div>
        <% end %>
        <div class="mb-4"></div>

        <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Save</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </form>

  <%# Blocking: on or off, its lists and allowlist, pausing %>
  <div class="card mb-4" id="dns-blocking">
    <div class="card-header d-flex align-items-center justify-content-between">
      <h5 class="mb-0"><%= lucide_icon('shield', size: 18) %> Blocking</h5>
      <% if !@blocking %>
        <span class="badge bg-secondary">Off</span>
      <% elsif @mode == 'off' %>
        <span class="badge bg-secondary">Waits for DNS</span>
      <% elsif @paused_until %>
        <span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">Paused</span>
      <% elsif @refreshing || @blocking_status.nil? %>
        <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">Updating the lists</span>
      <% elsif @blocking_status['error'] %>
        <span class="badge bg-danger">Not updated</span>
      <% else %>
        <span class="badge bg-success">On</span>
      <% end %>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="text-muted">
        Blocks ads, trackers and malware for every device that uses the NAS's DNS: a blocked name answers
        <code>0.0.0.0</code>, so what it would load never does. The lists come from the projects below and
        refresh every day.
      </p>

      <% if @blocking && @mode != 'off' %>
        <div class="dns-blocking-state mb-3">
          <% if @paused_until %>
            <%# The page looks again when the pause ends. %>
            <meta http-equiv="refresh" content="<%= (@paused_until - Time.now).ceil + 5 %>">
            <span>Paused for another <%= distance_of_time_in_words(Time.now, @paused_until) %>. Devices may take a minute to notice either way.</span>
            <%= button_to 'Resume now', dns_resume_blocking_path, method: :post, class: 'btn btn-sm btn-primary', form_class: 'd-inline' %>
          <% elsif @refreshing %>
            <%# The page looks again in a few seconds: a refresh takes about ten. %>
            <meta http-equiv="refresh" content="5">
            <span>Downloading the lists. This page shows what's blocked once they're in.</span>
          <% elsif @blocking_status.nil? %>
            <span>The lists haven't been downloaded yet. Save again to try now; they're also tried every day.</span>
          <% elsif @blocking_status['error'] %>
            <span class="text-danger">✗ The blocklist couldn't be updated: <%= @blocking_status['error'] %>.<%= ' The one before is still in use.' if @blocking_status['blocked'] %></span>
          <% else %>
            <span>Blocking <strong><%= number_with_delimiter(@blocking_status['blocked'].to_i) %></strong> domains, from lists downloaded
              <%= time_ago_in_words(Time.at(@blocking_status['refreshed_at'].to_i)) %> ago.</span>
            <%= button_to 'Pause for 5 minutes', dns_pause_blocking_path, method: :post, class: 'btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary', form_class: 'd-inline' %>
          <% end %>
        </div>
        <% (@blocking_status&.dig('lists') || {}).each do |key, list| %>
          <% next unless list['error'] %>
          <p class="small text-warning mb-1">✗ <%= DnsService::BLOCKLISTS.dig(key, :name) || key %> couldn't be downloaded (<%= list['error'] %>)<%= ', so its last copy is used' if list['downloaded_at'] %>.</p>
        <% end %>
      <% elsif @blocking %>
        <p class="small text-muted">Blocking works while DNS is on.</p>
      <% end %>

      <form action="<%= dns_update_blocking_path %>" method="post" id="dns-blocking-settings">
        <input type="hidden" name="_method" value="put">
        <input type="hidden" name="authenticity_token" value="<%= form_authenticity_token %>">
        <div class="form-check form-switch mb-3">
          <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox" role="switch" name="blocking" value="1" id="dns-blocking-on" <%= 'checked' if @blocking %>>
          <label class="form-check-label" for="dns-blocking-on">Block ads, trackers and malware</label>
        </div>

        <h6>Lists</h6>
        <div class="dns-choices dns-lists mb-4">
          <% DnsService::BLOCKLISTS.each do |key, list| %>
            <label class="dns-choice">
              <input type="checkbox" name="lists[]" value="<%= key %>" <%= 'checked' if @blocklists.include?(key) %>>
              <span class="dns-choice-title"><%= list[:name] %><%= ' (recommended)' if list[:default] %></span>
              <span class="dns-choice-text"><%= list[:about] %>
                <% if (count = @blocking_status&.dig('lists', key, 'domains')) %> <%= number_with_delimiter(count) %> domains.<% end %></span>
            </label>
          <% end %>
        </div>

        <h6>Allowlist</h6>
        <label class="form-label small text-muted" for="dns-allowlist">Domains never blocked, one a line: each one and the names under it get through, unless a list blocks one of those names itself. For a site a list breaks.</label>
        <textarea class="form-control mb-3 dns-allowlist" id="dns-allowlist" name="allowlist" rows="4" placeholder="example.com"><%= @allowlist.join("\n") %></textarea>

        <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Save</button>
      </form>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# Port forwards, while the NAS is the router %>
  <% if @mode == 'router' %>
  <div class="card mb-4" id="dns-forwards">
    <div class="card-header"><h5 class="mb-0"><%= lucide_icon('network', size: 18) %> Ports let in from the internet</h5></div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="text-muted small">A port on the NAS's internet address, passed to a device on the LAN: a game server, a camera. Everything else from the internet is dropped. Give the device a fixed address on <a href="<%= network_hosts_path %>">Network → Hosts</a> first, so the forward keeps pointing at it.</p>
      <% if @forwards.any? %>
        <table class="table table-sm dns-forwards mb-3">
          <thead><tr><th>From the internet</th><th>To</th><th>Name</th><th></th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <% @forwards.each do |forward| %>
              <tr>
                <td><code><%= forward.protocol %> <%= forward.port %></code></td>
                <td><code><%= @prefix %>.<%= forward.address %>:<%= forward.to_port %></code></td>
                <td><%= forward.name %></td>
                <td class="text-end"><%= button_to 'Remove', dns_destroy_forward_path(forward), method: :delete, class: 'btn btn-sm btn-outline-danger', form_class: 'd-inline' %></td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      <% end %>
      <form action="<%= dns_create_forward_path %>" method="post" class="row g-2 align-items-end">
        <input type="hidden" name="authenticity_token" value="<%= form_authenticity_token %>">
        <div class="col-6 col-md-2">
          <label class="form-label small" for="forward-port">Port</label>
          <input type="number" class="form-control" id="forward-port" name="port_forward[port]" min="1" max="65535" required>
        </div>
        <div class="col-6 col-md-2">
          <label class="form-label small" for="forward-protocol">Protocol</label>
          <select class="form-select" id="forward-protocol" name="port_forward[protocol]">
            <option value="tcp">TCP</option>
            <option value="udp">UDP</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <div class="col-6 col-md-3">
          <label class="form-label small" for="forward-address">To the device</label>
          <div class="input-group">
            <span class="input-group-text"><%= @prefix %>.</span>
            <input type="number" class="form-control" id="forward-address" name="port_forward[address]" min="2" max="254" required>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="col-6 col-md-2">
          <label class="form-label small" for="forward-to-port">Its port</label>
          <input type="number" class="form-control" id="forward-to-port" name="port_forward[to_port]" min="1" max="65535" placeholder="same">
        </div>
        <div class="col-8 col-md-2">
          <label class="form-label small" for="forward-name">Name</label>
          <input type="text" class="form-control" id="forward-name" name="port_forward[name]" maxlength="40" placeholder="optional">
        </div>
        <div class="col-4 col-md-1">
          <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary w-100">Add</button>
        </div>
      </form>
    </div>
  </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# IPv6: what the network announces, and whether devices learn the NAS %>
  <% if @mode != 'off' %>
  <div class="card mb-4" id="dns-ipv6">
    <div class="card-header d-flex align-items-center justify-content-between">
      <h5 class="mb-0"><%= lucide_icon('network', size: 18) %> IPv6</h5>
      <%= button_to 'Check now', dns_check_routers_path, method: :post, class: 'btn btn-sm btn-outline-secondary', form_class: 'd-inline' %>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="text-muted small">
        <% if @ipv6_address %>
          The NAS answers DNS over IPv6 at <code><%= @ipv6_address %></code><%= @ipv6_advertise ? ', which it announces' : ', from a range your router hands out' %>.
        <% else %>
          The NAS has no fixed IPv6 address for DNS on this network, so devices ask it over IPv4.
        <% end %>
        <strong>Check now</strong> asks the network's routers what they announce (it takes a few seconds).
      </p>

      <% if @ipv6_check %>
        <p class="small mb-2">Checked <%= time_ago_in_words(Time.at(@ipv6_check['checked_at'].to_i)) %> ago, on <%= @ipv6_check['interface'] %>:</p>
        <% if @ipv6_check['advertisers'].blank? %>
          <p class="small text-muted">No router answered. Devices here don't use IPv6 (or it's off), so they use the NAS's IPv4 address for DNS.</p>
        <% else %>
          <ul class="small dns-routers">
            <% @ipv6_check['advertisers'].each do |advert| %>
              <li>
                <code><%= advert['router'] %></code>
                <%= advert['lifetime'].to_i.zero? ? '(not a router)' : '(a router)' %>:
                <%= advert['prefixes'].any? ? "addresses in #{advert['prefixes'].join(', ')}" : 'no address range' %>;
                <%= advert['dns'].any? ? "DNS #{advert['dns'].join(', ')}" : 'no DNS server' %>
              </li>
            <% end %>
          </ul>
          <% if @other_ipv6_dns.any? %>
            <div class="alert alert-warning small mb-0">
              <% @other_ipv6_dns.each do |router, address| %>
                <code><%= router %></code> tells devices to use <code><%= address %></code> for DNS.
              <% end %>
              Devices may ask that instead of the NAS, going around its privacy and blocking. In that router's IPv6 (or
              DHCPv6) settings, set the DNS server it announces to <code><%= @ipv6_address || 'the NAS' %></code>, or have it
              announce none, so devices use the NAS's IPv4 address.
            </div>
          <% elsif @ipv6_address %>
            <p class="small text-success mb-0">✓ Devices learn only the NAS as their IPv6 DNS server.</p>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# How devices start using it, and how to undo it %>
  <div class="card mb-4" id="dns-how">
    <div class="card-header"><h5 class="mb-0"><%= lucide_icon('help-circle', size: 18) %> Using it</h5></div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <% server = @addresses.first || 'the NAS\'s address' %>
      <% if @mode == 'router' %>
      <ol class="mb-3">
        <li><strong>The provider's cable goes into <%= @wan_interface %></strong>, and your old router becomes an access point: its DHCP off, its own internet port empty, your devices on its LAN ports and Wi-Fi as before.</li>
        <li><strong>Devices get everything from the NAS</strong> as they renew: an address, the NAS as their router and DNS server. To hurry one along, turn its Wi-Fi off and on.</li>
        <li><strong>Check it:</strong> a device opens a website, and the top card says the NAS has an address from the provider.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-muted small mb-2"><strong>The way back:</strong> set this page to DNS + DHCP or Off, move the provider's cable back to your router, and turn its DHCP on again. While the NAS is off or restarting, the house has no internet: a System Update is a minute of that.</p>
      <% elsif @mode == 'dns_dhcp' %>
      <ol class="mb-3">
        <li><strong>Your router's DHCP is off</strong> (the NAS checked before it started). Leave the router's own address as it is.</li>
        <li><strong>Devices get an address from the NAS</strong> the next time they connect or renew. To hurry one along, turn its Wi-Fi off and on, or unplug its cable for a moment.</li>
        <li><strong>Check it:</strong> the device shows up on <a href="<%= network_index_path %>">Network → Leases</a>, and answers by its name, like <code>laptop.<%= @domain %></code>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-muted small mb-2"><strong>The way back:</strong> turn your router's DHCP on again, then set this page to DNS for my network or Off. Devices move back to the router as they renew; turning their Wi-Fi off and on hurries them. While the NAS is off, devices can't get a new address.</p>
      <% else %>
      <ol class="mb-3">
        <li><strong>Give the NAS a fixed address.</strong> In your router's DHCP settings, reserve <code><%= server %></code> for the NAS, so it never changes: every device will be pointed at it.</li>
        <li><strong>Point devices at it.</strong> For the whole house, set the DNS server in your router's settings to <code><%= server %></code> (often under Internet, WAN or DHCP). To try it first, set one computer's or phone's DNS server to <code><%= server %></code> by hand.</li>
        <li><strong>Check it:</strong> on that device, open a website, and look up <code><%= @nas_name %></code>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-muted small mb-2"><strong>The way back:</strong> set the router's DNS setting back to automatic (or the one device's back to automatic). While the NAS is off or restarting, the devices that use it can't look up names.</p>
      <% end %>
      <p class="text-muted small mb-0"><strong>Firefox</strong> is told to use this DNS instead of its own encrypted DNS. Other browsers and devices with their own encrypted DNS turned on go around it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

